title The hypothesis that declined the sweep, and the lap recovered out of the box
prompt THE BATON -- copal's autonomous lap. Lap N read 4083 by git rev-list --count HEAD at the open, so the rota row is 3 -- water, Venus, care and flow, the row that tastes. The round-open then adopted cf6a053ad and reported one record in the dead-letter box.
think The round-open printed two facts and the second one chose the lap: it adopted the anointed order, and one session log stood in the dead-letter box and nowhere else. Reading the stash showed a whole lap of my own -- a Rye repair, a witness, a control, a scan, a roster row and a REDS row -- proven on metal and dead at the send. Recovering it beats opening anything new, and the dead-letter guard says so out loud: the only way to turn it green is to put the record back in the channel.
think The number had to move before the work could land. The dead lap booked %503; the peer commit the round-open adopted, cf6a053ad, published %503 at 20260906.152240, nine minutes before my row's 20260906.154105. The derived spine gives the number to the earlier stamp, so the unshared row renumbers. reds_spine_derive_scan.sh --next answered 504, and after the row landed it answers 505 with rebindings=0.
think Recovered by file rather than by pop. The stash was cut against an elder head, so its construction/REDS.md carried a %499 row the peer had since closed and folded, and its README.md and docs-geode/libraries/README.md are hook output. Taking those whole would have regressed three pins. I took the five lane files and the roster hunk, and re-applied the REDS row by hand onto the current header.
think Re-proved everything before writing a claim about it, because the tree had moved under the work: scan ok, witness GREEN, control 22 of 22, and the three amphora delivery witnesses that actually build and run the module -- asker_reply, purchase_delivery, resin_chunk -- all GREEN. tame_style_check GREEN, width-check GREEN.
think Then the thing worth the lap. The scan's own note declined a wider sweep, and it declined it on a sentence about the rest of the tree: a sweep of every .rye would hold modules whose sockets are not UDP and whose reuse may be correct. That is a hypothesis, and it is one grep from being a measurement.
think Counted: 19 further modules carry setsockopt(SO_REUSEADDR). Every one opens SOCK_DGRAM. Not one names SOCK_STREAM anywhere in the file. The set the objection describes is empty on this tree. And the sites are not nineteen judgments -- open_socket was copied module to module carrying its comment intact, so all nineteen recite the same TCP sentence and the same REDS %282 citation word for word.
think What I could take and what I could not. Editing comlink, granary, eight linengrow and seven mantra modules is nineteen lanes' work. Widening my own scan is mine. So the third reading is REPORTED and never gated, it holds the elder objection by construction rather than by trust -- counting only files that open SOCK_DGRAM and never SOCK_STREAM -- and it reports the held-out set as tree_mixed_socket_files so the exclusion is visible rather than silent.
think The living leg asserts at least one rather than today's nineteen, and that is the design decision I most want the next reader to see: a pinned count would red this guard on the day another lane does the right thing, which is a guard punishing the repair. What it must prove is that the census ARRIVES -- %463's lesson, that a census answering zero of everything is indistinguishable from a healthy tree.
obs A HYPOTHESIS ABOUT THE REST OF THE TREE IS ONE GREP FROM BEING A MEASUREMENT, AND THE COMMENT THAT STATES IT READS EXACTLY LIKE A REASON. The scan's note was careful, plausible, and written by a lap that had just done good work -- and it bounded an instrument to a twentieth of its subject on a set that turns out to be empty. This is the row's own lesson one level up: an option carried for a plausible reason that does not apply is worse than one carried for no reason, because the comment beside it answers the question that would otherwise get asked. A scope declined for a plausible reason that does not apply is the same fault wearing the instrument's clothes.
obs THE COPY IS WHAT MAKES NINETEEN SITES ONE DEFECT. Nineteen independent authors reaching the same wrong conclusion would be a fact about UDP being confusing. Nineteen files carrying one function and one comment verbatim is a fact about copying, and it means the repair is one line nineteen times rather than nineteen judgments -- which is the difference between a class worth booking and a list worth ignoring.
obs A GUARD WITH NO STANDING MUST NOT GATE. The census reaches other seats' modules, so it reports and the verdict never moves for it -- proven by asserting the verdict beside every count in the control, since a reading that quietly gated would look identical in the count alone. A guard that reds Amphora over a file in Mantra is a guard the fleet turns off, and then the census is gone with it.
obs ONE NAMED ERROR CORRECTED WHILE THE FILE WAS OPEN: the getsockopt guard returned error.SetSockOptFailed, a name describing the call it replaced. It is error.GetSockOptFailed now, and the control's pen mirroring the repaired shape moved with it, so the pen still mirrors the module.
obs THE DEAD LAP'S OWN LOG LANDED AS WRITTEN, one field excepted. It had never reached a commit, so it is a draft rather than testimony, and correcting %503 to %504 before it first lands is honest where rewriting a landed log would not be. The correction is stated in the log itself rather than performed silently; every measurement it recorded stands as it was taken.
obs AND I DID THE SAME THING ONE PARAGRAPH LATER. The per-lane breakdown in the row and on the card first read *eight linengrow, seven mantra* -- I read it off the printed list rather than counting it. Piped through sort | uniq -c it is TEN linengrow, seven mantra, one granary, one comlink; the total nineteen was right and the parts were not. Caught before the send, while the roster pass I had to stop was running. The lap's whole subject is a plausible sentence standing where a count belongs, and it recurred inside the lap that booked it -- which is the honest measure of how cheap that fault is to commit.
obs THE STOPPED PASS RELEASED ITS LOCK. Killing the runner mid-flight to make the correction left construction/standing-equipment-run.lock.d absent and no pen behind, which is %487's trap repair proving itself on metal without being asked to.
loom witness=amphora_udp_reuseaddr wall_s=3 control_behaviors=32 peer_dgram_files=19 peer_dgram_sites=19 mixed_files=0
loom scan=amphora_udp_reuseaddr wall_ms=169 corpus_files=7 source_sites=0 kernel_binds=4
file tools/fixtures/a/amphora_udp_reuseaddr_scan.sh third reading added -- peer SOCK_DGRAM sites counted and reported, SOCK_STREAM files held out and named, never gated
file tools/fixtures/a/amphora_udp_reuseaddr_control.sh ten legs for the third reading, verdict asserted beside every count; 22 -> 32 behaviors
file tools/am/amphora_udp_reuseaddr_witness.rish three readings, one gate; the census is said aloud and asserted nowhere
file amphora/vessel_fetch_delivery.rye recovered from the box; getsockopt now returns error.GetSockOptFailed and the row citation reads %522
file tools/fixtures/a/amphora_vessel_port_lock.sh recovered -- header corrected to say it reaches one tree
file construction/REDS.md %522 booked BOOKED, renumbered off the anointed spine, with the recovery and the nineteen-module census appended
file construction/standing-equipment.kyri amphora_udp_reuseaddr seated tier lap, its placard renumbered
file construction/ITINERARY.md COPAL block replaced; card at 40,891 of 40,960
file session-logs/date/20260906/20260906-154353_the-option-that-silenced-the-collision.kyri the dead lap's own record, put back in the channel
obs AND THIS LOG WAS STASHED AT THE NEXT OPEN TOO, so the recovery lap died the same death as the lap it recovered. Neither record reached a commit until 20260906.161838 read both back out of the box, which is why the two file lines above now read %522 rather than %504: two more peers took %504 and %505 while both drafts sat in the stash. The stamp 20260906.154105 identified the row through every number it wore, which reached six before it landed as %522. Correcting a draft before it first lands is honest where rewriting a landed log would not be, and the correction is stated here rather than performed silently -- the same treatment this log gave the draft it recovered.
recommend check-in (Keaton) -- the nineteen peer modules are one line each plus their own witnesses, and they sit in four lanes I do not hold. Naming them on the card is what I can do; a sweep across four seats is a word rather than a lap.
